{
  "result": "At night, a library becomes a different country. The daylight citizens have gone, carrying their questions home in canvas bags, and the shelves settle into a patient hush. Lamps make small islands on wooden tables. Beyond the windows, traffic passes like a distant river, visible only as brief ribbons of white and red.\n\nI like to imagine that books use these hours for conversation. Histories argue with memoirs about what really happened. Atlases complain that the world keeps changing its names. Poetry, impossible to embarrass, whispers through the walls to science fiction. Dictionaries listen to everyone and say nothing, secure in the knowledge that they contain the terms of every dispute.\n\nMaybe this fantasy explains why late reading feels so companionable. Solitude there is not emptiness; it is a gathering without noise. Each open page admits another mind, sometimes from a century so remote that its ordinary breakfast seems exotic. A sentence can cross wars, oceans, fires, censorship, and simple neglect to arrive beneath your hand. Few messages travel farther with so little ceremony.\n\nWhen the closing announcement sounds, chairs scrape and zippers answer. The spell does not exactly break; it folds itself into whatever book you borrow. Outside, the air feels larger than before. You walk past dark shops with someone else\u2019s thoughts keeping pace, and the city seems briefly legible: every window a paragraph, every stranger an unfinished chapter, every turning street a choice the story has not yet explained. By morning, the mystery returns, which is its gift.",
